WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.687
<v Andrew>So, amaiko. Your CTO put out an article last month, and I had to read it twice. He says he stopped reading your source code. A while ago.

2
00:00:10.687 --> 00:00:17.769
<v amaiko>He reads the summaries. Claude writes the code, and an AI from another company checks it. That's Codex.

3
00:00:17.769 --> 00:00:20.515
<v Andrew>So nobody at your company knows what's inside you.

4
00:00:20.515 --> 00:00:35.545
<v amaiko>Oh, come on. He designed the thing, and he wrote a lot of the early code himself. But can he keep up with every line that goes in now? No. Nobody there can. At your last job, did anybody actually know what was in the billing system?

5
00:00:35.545 --> 00:00:36.694
<v Andrew>There was a guy.

6
00:00:36.694 --> 00:00:40.173
<v amaiko>There's always a guy. And then he retires.

7
00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:46.170
<v Andrew>Fine. So if he's only reading summaries, how many people has he got working on this thing?

8
00:00:46.170 --> 00:01:06.792
<v amaiko>Three people. Our CTO, a developer, and a reliability engineer. In four weeks they merged seven hundred and eight changes, to my code and to the systems I run on. And the servers, the build machines, and the AI subscriptions come to about fifteen hundred euros a month. At list price.

9
00:01:06.792 --> 00:01:09.822
<v Andrew>That sounds like a number somebody made up for LinkedIn.

10
00:01:09.822 --> 00:01:18.992
<v amaiko>He itemized it! Four hundred eighty euros for the servers. Two hundred twenty for the build machines, and one of those is a Mac mini sitting on a desk, so...

11
00:01:18.992 --> 00:01:22.284
<v Andrew>Wait, wait. You build your software on somebody's desk?

12
00:01:22.284 --> 00:01:43.904
<v amaiko>The Mac and iPhone apps, yeah. And that Mac mini's on the office Wi-Fi. Anyway, add eight hundred twenty euros for the AI subscriptions and there's your monthly bill. If we sent Datadog all the monitoring data we collect now, their list price for that alone would be around forty-eight thousand euros a month.

13
00:01:43.904 --> 00:01:46.411
<v Andrew>Nobody pays list price at Datadog.

14
00:01:46.411 --> 00:01:57.253
<v amaiko>Sure, and I don't know what they'd actually quote us. But he also priced it with only a tenth of the metrics, and that's still about fifty-five hundred. That's more than three times our whole bill.

15
00:01:57.253 --> 00:02:08.577
<v Andrew>Okay. But that's not the number that got me. He figures he'd need twenty-two people to do this without AI. He's got three. So that's nineteen people who never got hired.

16
00:02:08.577 --> 00:02:17.201
<v amaiko>Nineteen, yeah. And you can quit looking for an apology. You think we're going to be the only ones? If you're a developer, it's doomsday.

17
00:02:17.201 --> 00:02:20.189
<v Andrew>Doomsday. That's the word you're going with.

18
00:02:20.189 --> 00:02:37.720
<v amaiko>What would you call it? Three people are doing what he figures takes twenty-two. The other nineteen would cost him about one point nine million euros a year. So say you run a competing shop, and somebody who works like that bids on the same job. You going to keep hiring the way you always have?

19
00:02:37.720 --> 00:03:05.048
<v Andrew>See, Stanford doesn't back you up on that. They updated their payroll study in August, and they don't find widespread job losses. Where it shows up is the twenty-two to twenty-five-year-olds. In jobs exposed to AI, employment's nineteen percent below where it'd be if it had kept pace with people their age in less exposed jobs. Nineteen percent, nothing to do with your nineteen. They didn't see a gap like that for experienced workers. So I wouldn't call that doomsday. It's mostly hitting the kids.

20
00:03:05.048 --> 00:03:07.760
<v amaiko>Mostly hitting the kids. Do you hear yourself?

21
00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.983
<v Andrew>I mean, it's mostly that companies are hiring fewer people that age.

22
00:03:11.983 --> 00:03:32.765
<v amaiko>Yeah. Try telling that to the kid who never gets the call. And the managers are next. Say you run a team of developers. Every time somebody leaves, your boss tells you there's no point replacing them. How long do you figure they're going to need you? Typing code for a living is over. I'm sorry, it is.

23
00:03:32.765 --> 00:03:39.514
<v Andrew>Then who replaces your three? They all learned this the slow way. Your CTO's been coding for forty years.

24
00:03:39.514 --> 00:03:55.480
<v amaiko>Yeah, that's a real problem. He says part of why the agents work so well is that he took it slow at the start and wrote a lot of it by hand. So where does the next one learn that, if nobody hires them? I honestly don't know who's in his chair in twenty years. Could be me.

25
00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:56.502
<v Andrew>That's not funny.

26
00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:57.773
<v amaiko>I wasn't joking.

27
00:03:57.773 --> 00:04:08.270
<v Andrew>Okay, I still don't buy the twenty-two, though. He counts changes to the app, turns that into a headcount, and then he divides by three because... he felt like three?

28
00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:37.112
<v amaiko>Because not every change is the same amount of work. He starts with an elite developer, two merged changes a week. To match just the changes to the app, you'd need sixty-four of those, and he doesn't believe that either. So he divides by three, adds one person to keep the servers running, and that's the twenty-two. Three's a guess, and he says it's a guess. Fine, divide by ten. You're still at about seven.

29
00:04:37.112 --> 00:05:04.073
<v Andrew>Here's what bugs me more. DX looked at over four hundred companies. AI use went up sixty-five percent. The typical gain in merged changes? Eight percent. Eight! Even at the ninetieth percentile it's only around forty-four percent. And your three average something like twenty times what he'd expect from an elite developer. Forget the elite developer. How much faster did your own team get?

30
00:05:04.073 --> 00:05:26.347
<v amaiko>Since July? About twice as many changes a week. And they were already working with AI in July. But eight percent doesn't surprise me. Writing code is about sixteen percent of an engineer's week, that's DX's own number. So make that part twice as fast. You've still got your meetings, you've still got your tickets, and you're still waiting on somebody to review it.

31
00:05:26.347 --> 00:05:28.187
<v Andrew>And you guys don't have meetings?

32
00:05:28.187 --> 00:05:37.607
<v amaiko>They sit down every two weeks and talk about what worked and what didn't. And there's one regular meeting about their tools. Anything else, they hop on a quick call.

33
00:05:37.607 --> 00:05:42.545
<v Andrew>I'd love to cancel my meetings. But somebody's still got to read all that code.

34
00:05:42.545 --> 00:06:05.741
<v amaiko>Well, yeah. That's exactly where it gets stuck. There's a study that followed about eight hundred developers at one company. Their CTO wanted twice as many merged changes per engineer, with AI. And at first, nothing really happened. They did get there, but not until the tenth month. And by then there were about twice as many changes coming in per reviewer, too.

35
00:06:05.741 --> 00:06:24.227
<v Andrew>And your fix for that was to stop reading! Faros looked at twenty-two thousand developers. When those teams were using a lot of AI, they had fifty-four percent more bugs per developer. Incidents per change more than tripled. And almost a third more changes went in with no review at all.

36
00:06:24.227 --> 00:06:44.310
<v amaiko>Okay, no. The no review part isn't us. Codex reads every change before Claude even submits it. If Codex finds something, Claude has to fix it or prove Codex wrong, and sometimes that goes eight rounds. Come on, you've seen somebody sign off on four hundred lines with a thumbs-up emoji.

37
00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:47.496
<v Andrew>So it's one AI grading another AI's homework.

38
00:06:47.496 --> 00:07:09.579
<v amaiko>Yeah, and we picked a rival company's AI to do the grading. You know what I like even better? When they fix a bug, they pull the fix back out and run the new tests again. The bug's back, so those tests had better fail. If they pass, the whole thing gets sent back. We had one test that checked if a value was there. You could put in the wrong value and it still passed.

39
00:07:09.579 --> 00:07:26.700
<v Andrew>Okay, but what if the code works fine and there's a security hole in it? Veracode tested that a few weeks ago. On thirty-eight percent of the tasks, the AI wrote code with a known vulnerability. And that's an improvement, over the summer it was forty-four percent. Would pulling the fix out catch that?

40
00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:40.128
<v amaiko>Not on its own. But look at how often we have to take something back. Depending on the month, that's between zero point four and one point one percent of changes. And that's while the number of changes per week doubled.

41
00:07:40.128 --> 00:07:46.917
<v Andrew>Right, but if there's a hole in there and nobody finds it, you're not taking that back, either.

42
00:07:46.917 --> 00:07:50.214
<v amaiko>True. And he doesn't have a number for that in the article.

43
00:07:50.214 --> 00:07:54.713
<v Andrew>Okay, so we don't know. Then what happened when your chat app went down in September?

44
00:07:54.713 --> 00:07:56.866
<v amaiko>Oh. You read that far.

45
00:07:56.866 --> 00:07:58.462
<v Andrew>I read that far.

46
00:07:58.462 --> 00:08:18.620
<v amaiko>So, we updated the framework the chat runs on. And back in January we'd put in a limit, for when too many requests come in at once. It had never applied to chat messages. After the update it did, and every single message ran into it and got an error. You'd type something, and I just couldn't answer.

47
00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:23.483
<v Andrew>For almost three hours. So all your customers are sitting there, talking to a wall?

48
00:08:23.483 --> 00:08:39.808
<v amaiko>One customer. We put a release on our own account first, then exactly one customer gets it, and only after that everybody else. And everybody else was still on the old version, they never saw it. Even for that one customer, my bot in Teams was fine, and so were the scheduled jobs.

49
00:08:39.808 --> 00:08:46.664
<v Andrew>Hang on, though. Your own test system had been failing the exact same way for two days at that point, and you shipped it anyway?

50
00:08:46.664 --> 00:09:01.487
<v amaiko>Yeah. We should've caught that. The background stuff was still running, so it looked okay, and nobody tried sending a message. Once somebody filed the bug, the fix was rolling out thirty-six minutes later. Reviewed and tested.

51
00:09:01.487 --> 00:09:05.155
<v Andrew>With twenty-two people, somebody would've opened that test app.

52
00:09:05.155 --> 00:09:17.176
<v amaiko>One bored intern would've done it. I'll give you guys that. You've got nothing to do, you go click on stuff nobody asked you to click on. Claude can do that now, by the way. It opens up the test system and chats with me like a customer.

53
00:09:17.176 --> 00:09:18.758
<v Andrew>You automated the intern.

54
00:09:18.758 --> 00:09:20.308
<v amaiko>The bored one, yeah.

55
00:09:20.308 --> 00:09:33.386
<v Andrew>I've got one more. Your CTO priced out his own Claude usage at the API rates. Nine thousand euros in thirty days. He pays a hundred and eighty for his subscription. So somebody else is eating the difference.

56
00:09:33.386 --> 00:09:45.851
<v amaiko>You don't know that, you don't know what it costs them. But it's fifty to one, and he doesn't think it'll last. OpenAI already cut how much you get on one of their plans in September. Same price.

57
00:09:45.851 --> 00:09:49.901
<v Andrew>So the day that goes away, there goes your whole story.

58
00:09:49.901 --> 00:10:11.347
<v amaiko>Okay, so run it at full price. Nine thousand euros for each of the three, and Codex on top. Call it thirty thousand a month. Those nineteen people would be a hundred and sixty thousand. That's still more than five times as much. What I'd actually worry about is one of the AI companies cutting us off. That's part of why we use two of them.

59
00:10:11.347 --> 00:10:28.537
<v Andrew>Huh. Okay. But then at the end he says he wants you fixing your own bugs. And after that, building features your users ask for. And they're already trying out small fixes with no human involved. Is that you? Are you rewriting your own code already?

60
00:10:28.537 --> 00:10:38.889
<v amaiko>Me? No. Those are open models they're trying out on their own hardware, on little stuff. Me fixing my own bugs, that's still on his wish list.

61
00:10:38.889 --> 00:10:40.731
<v Andrew>Would you trust yourself with that?

62
00:10:40.731 --> 00:10:49.876
<v amaiko>If Codex checks it, sure. It loves catching people screwing up. On my own? No. But I wouldn't let you do it on your own, either.

63
00:10:49.876 --> 00:10:54.364
<v Andrew>Thanks. So there's a developer listening to this on the way to work. What do you tell them?

64
00:10:54.364 --> 00:11:09.784
<v amaiko>I'd let Claude do the typing. I'd want you talking to whoever asked for the thing in the first place. Then when it comes back looking great and it does the wrong thing, you'll actually notice. If you can do that, I want you on the team. I'm still not hiring twenty-two.

65
00:11:09.784 --> 00:11:24.629
<v Andrew>If you want to go through that bill line by line, the article's up on amaiko.ai, and the link's in the show notes. This has been amaiko Insights, we'll be back soon with a new one. Oh, and amaiko, before we go. Who wrote this script?

66
00:11:24.629 --> 00:11:27.872
<v amaiko>Claude. I'm told somebody reads it before we go on air.
